##DESCRIPTION
## A problem that asks which statement is true bsed on mean, median and mode.
##ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Exploratory data analysis/descriptive statistics)
## DBsection(Summary statistics)
## KEYWORDS('mean','median', 'mode', 'yoga')
## Level(2)

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'parserRadioButtons.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

$yr1 = random(25,35);
$yr2 = $yr1 + random(2,8);
$yr3 = $yr2 + random(2,8); 

$rb = RadioButtons(
    [ [
        "The oldest person in the class is $yr3 years old.",
        "Most people in the class are $yr2 years old.",
        "Each person in the class is either $yr1, $yr2, or $yr3 years old.",
        "At least half of the people in the class are $yr1 years old or younger."
    ] ],
    3,
    labels => 'ABC'
);

BEGIN_PGML

A yoga teacher collected the following data on the ages of the people in her class. 

- The median is [$yr1] years old.

- The mean is [$yr2] years old 

- The mode is [$yr3] years old

Which statement must be true? [_]{$rb}


END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
The statement "Most people in the class are [$yr2] years old " need not be true since 
the mean only tells us that the average age is [$yr2].  If half of the
class was [$yr2+1] and half was [$yr2-1] the mean would be [$yr2].

The statement "The oldest person in the class is [$yr3] years old " need not be true, the mode only tells us that no other age group has more people in the class than
the age group of [$yr3] year olds.

The statement "Each person in the class is either [$yr1], [$yr2] or [$yr3] years old " 
need not be true, indeed the only age group that is guaranteed to contain people is
the [$yr3] year old age group. 

The statement "At least half of the people in the class are [$yr3] years old or younger"
must be true since that is exactly what the statement "the median is [$yr3] years old" tells us.
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();

